  <div class="art-tag">Essays from the Studio</div>
  <h1 class="art-title">On Being Found Before You Are Searched</h1>
  <div class="art-meta">June 2026 · Berlin</div>

  <div class="art-body">
    <p>OBOSHI opens with a claim that isn't really about search engines: he already existed, in some sense, before the world began looking for him. The phrase 'thoughts become reality,' which he initially resisted as a demand to think correctly or hold the right frequency, comes to read instead as an instruction to simply receive and settle into a truth already present, rather than strain toward manifesting one.</p>
    <p>A dialogue with Claude explores why people struggle to believe they're already found rather than needing to earn discovery through proof, landing on the idea that existence itself doesn't require justification, and that this kind of belief works better held lightly than forced.</p>
    <p>He connects the idea directly to his own layered painting process, describing it as uncovering something already present rather than inventing something from nothing — acrylic, torn washi tape, and sheets of clear plastic layered and scraped back functioning as a way of letting what's already there surface.</p>
    <p>The essay closes by reframing a search engine as simply a tool for finding what already exists, concluding that if he already exists somewhere findable, all that's left is waiting for the world to search. The piece is dated by the author to Berlin, June 2026.</p>
  </div>

